WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:07.259
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:11.629
Now I'll eat my hat if this is going to be
two minutes, but I really hope you Fellow

00:00:11.629 --> 00:00:14.410
Scholars are going to like this little discussion.

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:19.080
Neil DeGrasse Tyson described a cool thought
experiment in one of his talks. He mentioned

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.090
that the difference of the human and the monkey
DNA is really small, a one digit percentage.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:30.070
For simplicity, let's say it is one percent.
For this one percent difference, there is

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:33.859
a huge difference in the intellect of humans
and apes.

00:00:33.859 --> 00:00:39.260
The smartest chimpanzee you can imagine can
do tasks like clapping his hands to a given

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:44.559
simple rhythm, or strike a match. Compared
to the average chimpanzee, such an animal

00:00:44.559 --> 00:00:49.660
would be an equivalent of Einstein or John
von Neumann. He can clap his hands. What is

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:55.020
that for humans? Children can do that! Even
before they start studying, they can effortlessly

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:00.090
do something that rivals the brightest minds
monkeys could ever produce.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:05.579
Imagine if there were a species that is the
same 1% difference away from us, humans in

00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:08.780
the same direction. What could they be capable of?

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:14.400
Their small children would be composing beautiful
symphonies, perfect harmonization for hundreds

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.820
of instruments, or they would be deriving
everything in the history of physics from

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:24.150
Newton's laws to quantum electrodynamics.
And their parents would be like: "oh, look

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:29.049
at what little Jimmy did, that's adorable!"
And they would put it on the fridge with a

00:01:29.049 --> 00:01:33.310
magnet. Just like we do with the adorable
little scribbles of our children.

00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:38.750
Just thinking about the possibilities gives
me chills. Now let's transition into neural

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:39.750
networks.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:44.409
An artificial neural network is a crude approximation
of the human brain that we can simulate on

00:01:44.409 --> 00:01:49.530
a computer to recognize images, paint in the
style of famous artists, or learn to play

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:53.470
video games and a number of different very useful things.

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:57.650
The number of connections that we can simulate
on the graphical card of our computer grows

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:02.430
closely to what's predicted in Moore's law,
which means that the computing capacity that

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:07.290
we have in our home computers doubles every
few years. It's pretty crazy if you think

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:11.590
about it, but most of you Fellow Scholars
have phones in your pockets that have more

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:15.319
computing capacity than NASA had to land on
the Moon.

00:02:15.319 --> 00:02:19.599
As years go by, there will be more and more
connections in these artificial neural networks,

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:24.900
and they don't have to adhere to stringent
constraints like our brains do, such as fitting

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:30.299
into a human cranium. A computer can be the
size of a building, or even bigger. Computers

00:02:30.299 --> 00:02:35.340
also transmit data with the speed of light,
which is way faster than the transfer capabilities

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.469
of the human brain.

00:02:36.469 --> 00:02:42.159
Nick Bostrom asked a lot of leading AI researchers
on the speed of progress in this field, and

00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:47.419
the conclusion of the study was basically
that the question is not can we achieve human-level

00:02:47.419 --> 00:02:50.669
intelligence, but when we'll achieve it.

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:55.370
However, the number of connections is not
everything as an artificial neural network

00:02:55.370 --> 00:03:00.779
is, by far not a 1:1 copy of the human brain.
We need something more than this.

00:03:00.779 --> 00:03:07.809
A very promising possible next frontier to
conquer is called recursive self-improvement.

00:03:07.809 --> 00:03:12.669
Recursive self-improvement means that we tell
the program to instead of work on an ordinary

00:03:12.669 --> 00:03:18.489
task like do better image recognition, we
would order it to work on improving its own

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:25.089
intelligence. Ask the program itself to rewrite
its code to be more efficient and more general.

00:03:25.089 --> 00:03:30.949
So we have a program with a ton of computational
resources working on getting smarter. And

00:03:30.949 --> 00:03:36.799
as it suddenly gets just a bit smarter, we
then have a smarter machine that can again

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:42.040
be asked to improve its own intelligence,
but it is now more capable of doing that,

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:47.089
therefore if we do this many times, leaps
are going to get bigger and bigger as an intelligent

00:03:47.089 --> 00:03:51.120
mind can do more to improve itself than an
insect can.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:56.439
This way, we may end up with an intelligence
explosion, which means a possible exponential

00:03:56.439 --> 00:04:01.650
increase in capabilities. And if this is the
case, talking about human-level intelligence

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:07.430
is completely irrelevant. During this process,
given enough resources, the system may go

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:13.939
from the intelligence of an insect to something
way beyond the capabilities of the most intelligent

00:04:13.939 --> 00:04:18.430
person who ever lived, ...in about a second or less.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:23.030
It could come up with way better solutions
in milliseconds than anything you've seen

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:26.889
on Two Minute Papers, and there's plenty of brilliant works out there.

00:04:26.889 --> 00:04:33.099
And of course, it could also develop never
before seen superweapons to unleash an unprecedented

00:04:33.099 --> 00:04:38.220
destruction on Earth. We wouldn't know if
it would do it, but it is capable of doing

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:43.550
that, which is quite alarming. I am not surprised
that Elon Musk compares creating an artificial

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:49.009
superintelligence to "summoning the demon",
and he offered 10 million dollars to research

00:04:49.009 --> 00:04:54.039
a safe way to develop this technology. Which
is obviously not nearly enough, but it is

00:04:54.039 --> 00:04:56.389
an excellent way to raise awareness.

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:01.759
Now, the classical argument on how to curb
such a superintelligence if one recognizes

00:05:01.759 --> 00:05:03.560
that it is up to no good.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:10.789
People say that, "well, I'll unplug it". The
problem is that people assume they can do

00:05:10.789 --> 00:05:11.229
it. Or maybe lock it away from the internet.

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:16.099
We can lock it up in any way we can think
of, but, there's only so much we can do, because

00:05:16.099 --> 00:05:21.849
as Neil DeGrasse Tyson argued, even the smartest
human who ever lived would be a blabbering,

00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:25.370
drooling idiot compared to such an intelligence.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:31.060
How easy is it for a grown adult to fool a
child? A piece of cake. The intelligence gap

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:36.240
between us and a superintelligence is more
than a thousand times that. It's even more

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:43.199
pathetic than a child or even a dog who tries
to fool us. We, humans can anticipate threats,

00:05:43.199 --> 00:05:48.840
like wielding weapons or locking dangerous
animals into cages, and so can superintelligent

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.120
beings also anticipate our threats, only way
better.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.849
It can trick you by pretending to be broken
and when the engineer goes there to fix the

00:05:57.849 --> 00:06:00.810
code, the manipulation can begin,

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:07.080
It could communicate with gravitational waves
or any kind of thing that we cannot even fathom,

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:10.259
just as an ant has no idea about our radio
waves.

00:06:10.259 --> 00:06:16.159
And we don't need to characterize superintelligent
beings as an adversary. The road to hell is

00:06:16.159 --> 00:06:21.610
paved with good intentions. It may very well
be possible that we assign it a completely

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:26.949
benign task that anyone could agree with,
and it would end up in a disaster in a way

00:06:26.949 --> 00:06:29.419
we cannot anticipate.

00:06:29.419 --> 00:06:34.389
Imagine assigning it the task of the maximizing
the number of paperclips. Nick Bostrom argues

00:06:34.389 --> 00:06:40.009
that it would at first, maybe create better
blueprints and factory lines. And after some

00:06:40.009 --> 00:06:45.409
point, it may run out of resources on earth,
then in order to maximize the number of paperclips,

00:06:45.409 --> 00:06:50.639
it would recognize that humans contain lots
of useful atoms, so eradicating humanity would

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:54.219
only be logical to maximize the number of
paperclips.

00:06:54.219 --> 00:06:59.400
Think about an other task: creating the best
approximation of the number pi -

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.779
one can approximate it to the most decimals
by using more resources, to have more resources,

00:07:04.779 --> 00:07:10.669
one builds more and bigger computers. At some
point, it runs out of space and eradicates

00:07:10.669 --> 00:07:14.110
humans because they are in the way of creating
more computers.

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:19.340
Or, it may eradicate humans way before that
because it knows they are capable of shutting

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:24.560
you down, and if you get shut down, there's
going to be less digits or paperclips, so

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:29.099
again, it's only logical to kill them - the
task will be done, but no one will be there

00:07:29.099 --> 00:07:31.169
anymore to say thank you.

00:07:31.169 --> 00:07:35.479
It is a bit like a movie where there is an
intelligent car, and the driver is in a car

00:07:35.479 --> 00:07:40.620
chase situation, shouting "we're too slow
and fuel is running out, please throw out

00:07:40.620 --> 00:07:46.719
all excessive useless weights", and along
some empty bottles, the person would be subsequently

00:07:46.719 --> 00:07:50.460
ejected from the vehicle.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:54.949
We don't know what is going to be the next
invention of mankind, but we know what's going

00:07:54.949 --> 00:08:02.180
to be the last one. Artificial superintelligence.
It has the potential to either eradicate humanity

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:08.529
or solve all of its problems. It is both the
deadliest weapon that will ever exist, and

00:08:08.529 --> 00:08:11.309
the key to eternal life.

00:08:11.309 --> 00:08:15.669
We need to be vigilant about the fact that
we have tons of money invested in artificial

00:08:15.669 --> 00:08:20.999
intelligence research, but barely any to make
sure we're doing it in a controlled and ethical

00:08:20.999 --> 00:08:26.199
way. This task needs some of the brightest
minds of our generation, and perhaps even

00:08:26.199 --> 00:08:27.240
the next one.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:32.820
And this needs to happen before we get there.
When we're there, it's already too late.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:39.029
I highly recommend an absolutely fantastic
article on Wait But Why about this, or Nick

00:08:39.029 --> 00:08:43.610
Bostrom's amazing book, Superintelligence.
There are tons of other reading materials

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:47.639
in the description box for the more curious
Fellow Scholars out there.

00:08:47.639 --> 00:08:51.309
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

